          html: `<p>Generally, apartments have different garbage categories that must be disposed of separately. German waste management normally offers different garbage containers.</p>
          <p><strong>Recyclable waste</strong> (marked with the Grüner Punkt logo) — disposed of in yellow bags known as the <em>Gelber Sack</em> (empty milk cartons, plastic bags, etc.). In students' residences the recyclable waste is not separated, so you don't need yellow bags. For collection, hand the bags over a "collection card" — find out which day via the "waste calendar" (distributed at the end of the year, or obtainable at the Rathaus/Bürgercenter or recycling centers).</p>
          <p><strong>Biodegradable waste / Biomüll</strong> — food waste, kept and disposed of separately in the brown container.</p>
          <p><strong>Glass</strong> — recyclable, disposed of by color (green, brown, white) into public glass-recycling containers. In the university neighborhood these are on Paul-Ehrlich-Straße.</p>
          <p><strong>Paper</strong>, cardboard, etc. — disposed of separately in the blue container for paper/Altpapier.</p>
          <p><strong>Non-recyclable garbage / Restmüll</strong> — collected in green containers, usually not emptied every day, but once every two weeks on a specified day. The Gelber Sack is collected on special days per schedule — ask your janitor.</p>
          <p>For <strong>electronic scrap</strong> (fridges, computers) or <strong>bulk waste</strong> (furniture), inform your janitor or landlord to arrange a free pick-up date with the city's waste management team.</p>`
